{"v":1,"ts":"2026-07-29T00:16:00Z","actor":"code_review","type":"accepted_residual","ref_id":"auto-update-config","finding":"Two cross-process TOCTOU races in update-check.mjs on the opt-in auto path: RR-1 the running-marker concurrent-sync guard (read->decide->spawn->write has no OS lock, so N truly-simultaneous same-repo session starts each spawn a detached aai-update sync); RR-2 once-only outcome surfacing can print the applied line more than once under the same window (cosmetic).","decision":"ACCEPTED AS DOCUMENTED RESIDUAL, merge now + fast-follow. Justification: bounded blast radius (aai-update isolates each run in a mktemp clone per SPEC-0052 and applies identical upstream bytes via per-file cp -a, so concurrent syncs converge to a single coherent state with no corruption; RR-2 is purely cosmetic), outside Spec-AC-09 which guarantees only the sequential subsequent-run case (TEST-016 green), and a rare real-world trigger (two AAI sessions starting in the SAME repo within the ~tens-of-ms read->spawn window, both outside the 24h throttle). Fast-follow: atomic O_EXCL wx claim on a separate .aai/cache/update-sync.lock so a losing racer backs off; same claim covers RR-2.","source":"code review of PR feat/auto-update-config (spec-auto-update-config), re-validation empirically reproduced 5-parallel->5-sync convergence"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-07-29T14:45:00Z","actor":"remediation","type":"remediation_decision","ref_id":"update-sync-atomic-lock","finding":"Independent validation PASSed the cold-start O_EXCL claim but reproduced a residual on the CONCURRENT stale-lock RECLAIM path of acquireSyncLock in .aai/scripts/update-check.mjs: two racers both pass the staleness read, then the loser's rmSync deletes the winner's freshly-recreated lock and its create succeeds -> TWO detached aai-update syncs for one stale lock (RR-1 not fully closed beyond cold start).","decision":"CLOSED atomically. Two root causes fixed: (1) the reclaim rm-then-create was not serialized (a lone O_EXCL create cannot arbitrate a reclaim that must first remove the existing lock) -> reclaim is now serialized behind a short-lived EXCLUSIVE reclaim lock (update-sync.lock.reclaim) whose sole holder re-checks staleness AFTER gaining exclusivity, so it never acts on a fresh lock a prior reclaimer installed; (2) openSync('wx')+writeSync left a torn (created-but-empty) window in which a concurrent reader mtime-ages the lock as stale whenever the caller's clock differs from the file's real mtime (e.g. an injected --now), falsely triggering recovery -> eliminated by creating locks with FULL content atomically (writeFile temp then linkSync). New tests: TEST-027 (amplified true-parallel reclaim of one pre-existing stale lock, exactly one spawn/invocation; RED reproduced 14 spawns for 10 rounds, post-fix 30x isolated deterministic) and TEST-028 (surfaceOutcome restore-on-post-claim-read-failure, Defect 2). RR-1 now closed for cold-start AND concurrent reclaim; RR-2 (surface once) unchanged.","residual":"One precisely-scoped sliver remains, strictly rarer than the original RR-1: if a reclaimer CRASHES in the ~microsecond window while holding the reclaim lock AND >30min later TWO racers recover that abandoned reclaim lock with sub-microsecond interleave, both could reclaim -> two syncs. Bounded/converges (aai-update isolates each in a mktemp clone applying identical upstream bytes, per the original RR-1 accepted-residual reasoning). Requires a crash + 30min + simultaneity, versus the original RR-1 which needed only two simultaneous starts.","source":"remediation of CHANGE-DRAFT-update-sync-atomic-lock; docs/ai/tdd/red-20260729T141859Z-update-sync-atomic-reclaim.log -> green-20260729T144345Z-update-sync-atomic-reclaim-full-suite.log"}
{"v": 1, "ts": "2026-07-29T16:25:00Z", "actor": "remediation", "type": "remediation_decision", "ref_id": "update-sync-atomic-lock", "finding": "5d bot-sweep of the atomic-lock code (CHANGE-0093) surfaced 4 valid findings on .aai/scripts/update-check.mjs: (A/Codex P2) claimLockFile's fs.linkSync throws EPERM/ENOSYS/EOPNOTSUPP/EMLINK on filesystems that disallow hard links, and acquireSyncLock treated ANY failure as 'cannot claim' -> returned false -> the caller reported a bogus 'sync in progress' so auto-update SILENTLY never ran on such a host; (B/Codex P2) releaseSyncLock deleted the lock by PATH, so a sync outliving the 30min stale window would delete a second run's reclaimed lock and let a third sync start; (C/Codex P2) a reporter killed after surfaceOutcome's claim rename (outcome -> .surfacing.PID.SEQ) orphaned the sole outcome copy with the real path gone -> it never surfaced; (D/Copilot) the ~334 comment and CHANGELOG described the claim as openSync('wx') though the impl uses linkSync.", "decision": "Fixed all four. (A) claimLockFile now falls back to atomic openSync(target,'wx')+writeSync when linkSync reports hard-links-unsupported (EEXIST on either path = held; any other errno = genuine error); acquireSyncLock/acquireReclaimLock return a {claimed|held|error} enum and main() surfaces a genuine 'error' as a loud non-fatal stderr WARNING + skip, never a silent 'in progress'. (B) the lock body now carries a unique owner token and releaseSyncLock removes the lock only when the on-disk token matches (child receives it via --lock-token). (C) surfaceOutcome recovers the newest mtime-STALE orphaned .surfacing.* when the outcome is absent (a fresh orphan from a live reporter is never resurrected). (D) comment + CHANGELOG rewritten to describe linkSync-with-wx-fallback. Tests TEST-029/030/031 added (suite now 31, deterministic, 3x green); TEST-028's orphan assertion moved from the stale shared '.surfacing' path to the per-pid '.surfacing.*' glob so it catches a real per-pid orphan regression.", "residual": "The pre-existing sub-microsecond crash-window residual (a reclaimer crashing while holding the exclusive reclaim lock, then two racers recovering the abandoned reclaim lock >30min later with sub-us interleave -> two syncs) is UNCHANGED by these fixes \u2014 it is orthogonal to A/B/C and remains bounded/convergent per the 2026-07-29T14:45 entry. Finding A's wx fallback re-introduces the tiny created-but-empty torn-lock window ONLY on hard-link-unsupported filesystems (documented, bounded); on normal filesystems the linkSync path keeps the torn window closed, so the residual is not enlarged there.", "source": "5d bot-sweep remediation of CHANGE-0093; docs/ai/tdd/red-20260729T161802Z-update-sync-botfix.log -> green-20260729T162137Z-update-sync-botfix.log"}
