{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-19T14:36:23Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-tripwire-stale-line-blind-to-d7","ref_id":"suites-must-not-touch-the-shipping-repo","severity":"P3","finding":"the ratchet's STALE line cannot tell 'this suite is fixed' from 'D7 masked its write because the path was already dirty'","decision":"on the 2026-08-19 full framework run over a tree where docs/INDEX.md and docs/ai/overview.* were already modified, all four seeded entries printed STALE although all four are live writers; the line carries an 'if that holds on a clean tree' caveat, but a reader draining the list from a local dirty-tree run would delete entries that are still needed","source":"scratchpad framework.log run test-20260819-134731 aggregate, four STALE lines with zero tripwire violations"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-19T15:28:16Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-tripwire-ratchet-path-glob-widens","ref_id":"suites-must-not-touch-the-shipping-repo","severity":"P3","finding":"tripwire_path_listed loops over an unquoted $2, so a ratchet path list undergoes word-splitting and pathname expansion; an entry reading docs/* covers whatever the glob matches in the framework's CWD at run time","decision":"an allowlist entry must mean exactly the paths it names; a glob makes its meaning depend on where the framework was started, measured as ALLOWED from the repo root and FAIL from /","source":"validation round 2, suites-must-not-touch-the-shipping-repo, 2026-08-19; no shipped entry uses a glob and the wrong-cwd direction fails closed"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-19T15:28:16Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-followups-json-truncated-on-pipe","ref_id":"suites-must-not-touch-the-shipping-repo","severity":"P2","finding":"follow-ups.mjs list --json stops at exactly 65536 bytes when stdout is a pipe, emitting invalid JSON, while the same command redirected to a file completes","decision":"every caller that pipes the registry into a parser gets a silent truncation the moment the ledger passes 64 KB; measured 56549 bytes on 2026-08-19 so it does not bite yet, which is precisely why it will surprise someone later","source":"validation round 2, 2026-08-19; Unterminated string at position 65522 on the piped form, 105401 bytes and valid to a file"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-19T15:28:24Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-tripwire-ratchet-duplicate-entry","ref_id":"suites-must-not-touch-the-shipping-repo","severity":"P3","finding":"tripwire_allowlist_entry returns on the first match, so a second ratchet entry naming the same suite is dead code and nothing warns that the two collide","decision":"the ratchet is meant to drain visibly, and a silently dead entry can make a reviewer believe a path is covered when an earlier entry actually governs","source":"validation round 2, 2026-08-19; both orders measured, narrow-first fails the wide write and wide-first allows it"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-19T16:11:17Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-tripwire-fail-hides-suite-log-tail","ref_id":"suites-must-not-touch-the-shipping-repo","severity":"P2","finding":"a suite that fails on its OWN exit code AND trips the tripwire prints only the tripwire block, so the failure-lines-plus-tail dump that makes a CI log diagnosable is lost","decision":"run_test routes on one outcome value: a non-empty tw_fail_kind rewrites outcome to 'tripwire', so the '*' branch that greps the whole log for failure markers and tails 30 lines never runs for that suite; the reader sees a tripwire report and no reason for the exit code","source":"code review of tests/skills/test-framework.sh run_test outcome switch, 2026-08-19 remediation pass for spec-suites-must-not-touch-the-shipping-repo"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-19T16:11:17Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-tripwire-git-internals-unnamed","ref_id":"suites-must-not-touch-the-shipping-repo","severity":"P3","finding":"writes to .git internals (git config --local, hooks, refs, the stash, reflog) are invisible to the tripwire and are not named as a limit beside D3 and D7","decision":"the snapshot is HEAD plus git status --porcelain=v1 plus, now, a content hash of the ratchet paths; none of the three observes .git, so a suite that rewrites a hook or drops a stash entry in the shipping repo reads clean and the header claims no such bound","source":"code review of .aai/scripts/lib/repo-tripwire.sh contract header, 2026-08-19 remediation pass"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-19T16:11:17Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-suite-map-tripwire-row-incomplete","ref_id":"suites-must-not-touch-the-shipping-repo","severity":"P3","finding":"the suite-map row for aai-repo-tripwire omits tests/skills/test-aai-spec-lint.sh and .aai/system/PROFILES.yaml, both of which this scope changed and the suite depends on","decision":"selection by changed files will not pick the tripwire suite when only those two paths move, so the guard's own gate can be skipped by the selector that exists to run it","source":"code review of tests/skills/suite-map.yaml against the spec's inline review scope, 2026-08-19 remediation pass"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-19T16:11:17Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-wrapper-tripwire-snapshot-leak","ref_id":"suites-must-not-touch-the-shipping-repo","severity":"P3","finding":"the mktemp snapshot files .aai/scripts/aai-run-tests.sh creates for the tripwire leak on a signal because the script installs no trap to remove them","decision":"the wrapper is the watchdog path: a timeout or an interrupt kills it before the cleanup line, leaving two files per run in TMPDIR on the machines that run tests most often","source":"code review of .aai/scripts/aai-run-tests.sh tripwire arming, 2026-08-19 remediation pass"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-19T16:59:51Z","actor":"owner","type":"hitl_decision","ref_id":"suites-must-not-touch-the-shipping-repo","finding":"An independent cold diagnosis (Fable) plus a reproduced experiment established that the repo test corpus verifies token presence, not meaning: test-aai-constitution.sh stays fully green when CONSTITUTION article 6 is inverted to say STATE.yaml may be hand-edited, with only the pinned tokens preserved. Reproduced by the orchestrator in a scratch clone, 10/10 arms PASS, exit 0. The same diagnosis found there is no product tree in this repo and the sole downstream consumer aai-hermes is pinned at 2026-08-03 with Template version UNKNOWN while releases ran to 2026.08.16.","decision":"Owner decision 2026-08-19: FINISH the tripwire ride and land it, THEN change direction. The tripwire is therefore explicitly TRANSITIONAL. The recommended successor is to run every suite in a disposable worktree, which removes the cause; when that lands, the tripwire, its known-offender ratchet and the ratchet-path hashing are to be DELETED and every follow-up about them closed as moot. Recording this so the tripwire is not mistaken for permanent architecture and maintained by someone later.","source":"orchestrator + fable cold diagnosis, 2026-08-19; constitution inversion reproduced in scratchpad/const-probe"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-19T17:51:52Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-parallel-roles-dirty-the-tree","ref_id":"suites-must-not-touch-the-shipping-repo","severity":"P2","finding":"dispatching a code review and a validation concurrently makes the review's own report file land in the checkout mid-run, which the tripwire correctly reports as a suite dirtying the shipping repo","decision":"measured 2026-08-19: the 80-suite aggregate carried one failure (aai-deslop) caused entirely by the parallel reviewer writing docs/ai/reviews/... at 17:12Z; the roles are read-only by contract but their REPORTS are not, so parallel dispatch is unsafe while any framework run is in flight","source":"validation round 3 of suites-must-not-touch-the-shipping-repo; aai-deslop re-ran clean standalone"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-19T18:01:31Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-tripwire-allowed-ignores-pre-dirty","ref_id":"suites-must-not-touch-the-shipping-repo","severity":"P2","finding":"the ratchet entry path-subset test compares only paths that MOVED, so an allowlisted suite writing an already-dirty non-ratchet path still reads tripwire ALLOWED inside its listed paths at exit 0","decision":"validation F4 (suggested id fu-tripwire-attested-clean-ignores-pre-dirty-paths, 50 chars, over the 40-char id limit, so filed under this shorter id) and code review NB-1 both measured it: with a pre-existing M docs/other.md an allowlisted suite writing its listed path AND docs/other.md passes with the out-of-entry write landed. Documented as a stated bound in spec D8 and in the vendored library header this pass, not enforced, because on a clean start a non-ratchet path only becomes pre-dirty after an earlier suite already failed the run, so it degrades a red run's offender list rather than turning CI green; mooted by the recorded disposable-worktree successor","source":"docs/ai/validation/validation-20260819T174800Z-suites-must-not-touch-the-shipping-repo-round3.md F4; docs/ai/reviews/review-dual-verdict-suites-must-not-touch-the-shipping-repo-20260819T171200Z.md NB-1 fixture fxC"}
