{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-19T18:47:20Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-suggested-ids-read-as-filed","ref_id":"suites-must-not-touch-the-shipping-repo","severity":"P2","finding":"validation reports list follow-up ids under a 'filed:' key when the role only SUGGESTED them, so the orchestrator relays them as filed and they never enter the ledger","decision":"measured 2026-08-19: two ids I passed into a dispatch as already filed were absent from decisions.jsonl, and one exceeded the 40-char id cap so it could never have been filed under that name; a finding that reads as recorded but is not is worse than one openly dropped","source":"remediation of code-review BLOCKING-3, 2026-08-19; both ids traced back to round-3 validation's report"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-19T19:24:46Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-tripwire-unavailable-discards-hash","ref_id":"suites-must-not-touch-the-shipping-repo","severity":"P3","finding":"the clean-to-dirty escalation lifts only the 'clean' state, so hash evidence is discarded when tw_state is 'unavailable', and the lost-snapshot block names no paths","decision":"a run that loses its snapshot mid-suite already fails closed, but it throws away the one piece of evidence that would tell the reader WHICH path was written","source":"final code review NB-3, suites-must-not-touch-the-shipping-repo, 2026-08-19"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-19T19:24:46Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-tripwire-contract-omits-hash-side","ref_id":"suites-must-not-touch-the-shipping-repo","severity":"P3","finding":"the vendor-facing CONTRACT block in repo-tripwire.sh omits aai_tripwire_hash_usable and aai_tripwire_hasher and states no hash-side limit","decision":"downstream projects vendor this file and read only its header; cksum is CRC32 and an UNREADABLE path compares equal on both sides, neither of which the contract mentions","source":"final code review NB-4, 2026-08-19"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-19T20:31:17Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-tripwire-fixture-dirs-leak","ref_id":"suites-must-not-touch-the-shipping-repo","severity":"P3","finding":"tests/skills/test-aai-repo-tripwire.sh new_fixture is always called in a command substitution, so its WORKDIRS+= runs in a subshell and the EXIT trap sees an empty array","decision":"every full run of the suite leaves its temp git repositories behind under TMPDIR (13 of them after the TEST-007 arms added here); the trap reads as cleanup but removes nothing","source":"Codex P2 inline review on PR #266, tests/skills/test-aai-repo-tripwire.sh:121"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-19T20:31:17Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-framework-rundir-same-second","ref_id":"suites-must-not-touch-the-shipping-repo","severity":"P2","finding":"tests/skills/test-framework.sh derives RUN_DIR from a second-resolution RUN_ID, so two framework processes started in the same second share one results dir and overwrite each other's per-suite tripwire snapshots","decision":"process B can overwrite A's before-snapshot with an already-dirty porcelain state, so both comparisons read clean and the guard is defeated","source":"Codex P2 inline review on PR #266, tests/skills/test-framework.sh:363"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-19T20:40:16Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"process_finding","ref_id":"registry-triage-20260819","finding":"CORRECTION to a figure relayed to the owner on 2026-08-19. The cold diagnosis reported ~2798 grep-style assertions as evidence that the suite corpus verifies token presence rather than behaviour. I attempted to verify that classification twice and BOTH attempts were unsound: the first treated every target path containing a dollar sign as a fixture, but a variable can point at a real repository document (test-aai-constitution.sh greps the real CONSTITUTION through DOC_FILE); the second resolved simple variable assignments and still missed composed ones, so it undercounted again and still excluded the one suite where the defect is already proven.","decision":"Stop counting. A grep census is itself a proxy for the claim. The direct measurement is the inversion experiment: rewrite a document sentence to assert the opposite while preserving the pinned tokens, then run the owning suite and record RED or GREEN. One case is proven (CONSTITUTION article 6, suite fully green). The 2026-08-20 first task is ten more inversions sampled deterministically, not a refined census. Neither 2798 nor my 11 nor my 28 should be quoted as the rate.","source":"orchestrator self-check 2026-08-19 ~20:40Z; sampler kept at scratchpad/sample-prose-pins.mjs, seed 20260820"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-19T22:14:35Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"kpi_review","ref_id":"registry-triage-20260819","finding":"Falsification experiment run 2026-08-19/20, seed 20260820, ten prose-pin assertions across ten different suites, each inverted to assert the OPPOSITE of what the document said while preserving every pinned token. RESULT: 10 GREEN out of 10, zero suites caught a semantic reversal; pass counts byte-identical to baseline in all ten runs. ARMED-CHECK: the same ten, mutated to break only the TOKEN while keeping the meaning, all went RED naming the targeted assertion. So these assertions are perfectly sensitive to spelling and perfectly blind to claims. SCOPE CORRECTION, and it cuts against the pessimistic reading: the eligible prose-pin layer is 308 assertions out of 3001 (about 10 percent); the other 90 percent are fixture-driven or execute real code and this experiment says nothing about them. The cold diagnosis figure of ~2798 and my own paraphrase that the corpus is largely decoration were BOTH overstated.","decision":"The defect is real, measured, and confined to the prose-pin layer. Do not delete or demote behavioural arms. Two further conclusions carry weight: (1) adding more required phrases does NOT convert a presence check into a meaning check - one assertion required seven tokens including a full clause and still passed, because every token can be quoted inside a sentence that repudiates it, so the instinct to pin more phrases is wrong; (2) a negative literal pin is the weakest shape available - it is blind to the reversal AND fires on a harmless paraphrase. Next action is to decide what the prose-pin layer should become (deleted, or demoted to advisory), not to strengthen it.","source":"scratchpad/falsify2 - resolve-pins.mjs, select.mjs, edits.mjs, controls.mjs and per-row logs; repository untouched, HEAD 985d2b6 before and after"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-19T23:10:13Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"process_finding","ref_id":"suites-must-not-touch-the-shipping-repo","finding":"Feasibility probe 2026-08-19/20 of running every suite in a disposable git worktree. IT WORKS: 13 of 13 sampled suites pass under isolation, including all four known writers, which write into the throwaway copy; the shipping tree returns an empty porcelain afterwards. No suite needed editing, because every suite resolves PROJECT_ROOT from its own path. Measured cost +0.37s per suite, about +30s on a full run (~1.4 percent). Deletable surface if the tripwire goes: ~1140 lines (repo-tripwire.sh 305, test-aai-repo-tripwire.sh 713, ~104 in test-framework.sh, ~20 in aai-run-tests.sh). CORRECTION to the cold diagnosis: its ~15-line estimate is exact for the patch it described, but that patch checks out HEAD, so uncommitted edits and brand-new suites are invisible and a TDD RED can never go red - it fails silently. The correctness-complete patch that replays the working-tree diff and copies untracked files is 43 lines and costs 0.04s.","decision":"Proceed, with two changes to the plan. (1) Budget 43 lines, not 15. (2) .aai/scripts/aai-run-tests.sh is IN SCOPE, not a follow-up: it wraps an arbitrary command and roles use it ad hoc, so until it is covered the tripwire must NOT be deleted - it is the only thing catching that path today. TOP RISK to prove before shipping: docs/ai/STATE.yaml is gitignored and absent from a worktree, so four assertion groups (check-state TEST-010 and TEST-002, orchestration-mode TEST-016, the orchestration-dispatch repo-wide gate) silently become PASSING SKIPS - a greener run that tests less, the exact failure mode the tripwire was built against. The seeding fix is written and syntax-checked but NOT verified.","source":"scratchpad probe, 2026-08-19/20; repository untouched, HEAD 6f702bf before and after; unmeasured: a full 80-suite run either way, the seeding fix under test, CI/Linux, the PowerShell and WSL paths, parallel execution, and 67 of ~80 suites"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-20T11:43:52Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"verification","ref_id":"suites-must-not-touch-the-shipping-repo","finding":"The probe named one thing to prove before shipping worktree isolation: docs/ai/STATE.yaml is gitignored and absent from a worktree, so four assertion groups silently become PASSING SKIPS. Measured 2026-08-20 by the orchestrator directly, not delegated. UNSEEDED worktree: check-state prints PASS: check-state real-repo check skipped (no STATE.yaml) and PASS: template/live header-parity check skipped, orchestration-mode prints SKIP for TEST-016, orchestration-dispatch prints the repo-wide check-state gate SKIPPED twice. SEEDED worktree (copy STATE.yaml plus LOOP_TICKS.jsonl from the base; hitl-channel.json does not exist here): all four restored - Real repo STATE.yaml has no duplicate top-level key, Template schema header byte-equals the live header, STATE.yaml schema header documents the optional orchestration block, and the repo-wide gate runs. Zero genuine skips remain; the one residual SKIP token is a false positive of my own grep matching the word SKIPPED inside a PASS message, and the LIVE tree prints the same single line, so seeded worktree and live tree behave identically.","decision":"The last unknown blocking the worktree-isolation ride is closed. The seeding step is required and is three file copies. Remaining unmeasured items from the probe are unchanged and none of them block a start: a full 80-suite run either way, CI/Linux behaviour, the PowerShell and WSL paths, parallel execution, and 67 of ~80 suites.","source":"orchestrator, 2026-08-20 ~23:15Z; scratchpad/seedtest with wt-noseed and wt-seed worktrees off a re-baselined copy; real repo untouched at HEAD 4710320"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-20T13:31:00Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-ismain-symlink-realpath","ref_id":"suites-run-in-a-disposable-worktree","severity":"P2","finding":"every .mjs CLI guards main() with path.resolve(process.argv[1]) === fileURLToPath(import.meta.url), which is FALSE whenever the checkout is reached through a symlink, so the CLI exits 0 having printed nothing","decision":"a silent no-op at exit 0 is indistinguishable from success to every caller; measured on macOS where TMPDIR lives under /var (a symlink to /private/var), orchestration-dispatch.mjs produced empty stdout at exit 0 and three hitl-propagation arms read as assertion failures. Worked around here by realpathing the disposable checkout, not fixed at the guard","source":"measured 2026-08-20 in a disposable worktree under macOS TMPDIR; fixed locally by pwd -P in tests/skills/test-framework.sh iso_create and .aai/scripts/aai-run-tests.sh"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-20T13:31:00Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-isolation-seeding-duplicated","ref_id":"suites-run-in-a-disposable-worktree","severity":"P3","finding":"the three-step working-tree seeding (tracked diff replay, untracked-not-ignored copy, gitignored per-dev seed) is implemented twice, once in tests/skills/test-framework.sh and once in .aai/scripts/aai-run-tests.sh","decision":"two copies of the subtle half of the design can drift, and only one of them is vendored downstream; a shared POSIX library beside .aai/scripts/lib/repo-tripwire.sh would be DRY but costs a PROFILES.yaml classification and a test-aai-spec-lint.sh branch-diff allowlist entry, which is out of this ride scope","source":"docs/specs/SPEC-DRAFT-spec-suites-run-in-a-disposable-worktree.md D1/D2; the duplicated blocks"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-20T13:31:00Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-docsaudit-t003-red-on-new-doc","ref_id":"suites-run-in-a-disposable-worktree","severity":"P3","finding":"test-aai-docs-audit.sh TEST-003 regenerates docs/INDEX.md and diffs it against the committed one, so it is RED for the whole life of any scope that adds a doc, and test-aai-delta-stage3.sh TEST-007 inherits the failure","decision":"the full framework aggregate cannot be green while a normal in-flight intake or spec exists, which trains readers to discount two standing failures; proven pre-existing on a pre-change clone where the same arm fails with only the intake draft present and passes with it moved aside","source":"measured 2026-08-20 on a pre-change clone at 2372805: aai-docs-audit FAIL with the untracked CHANGE draft present, PASS with it moved aside"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-20T14:04:32Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-orchestrator-monitor-uses-gnu-find","ref_id":"suites-run-in-a-disposable-worktree","severity":"P2","finding":"the orchestrator's liveness check for running agents used GNU find syntax (-newermt '-N minutes') with stderr sent to /dev/null; on this host find rejects it, so the check reported zero writes as a RESULT when it was a silent command failure","decision":"on 2026-08-20 that fabricated zero was the sole evidence for concluding a subagent had stalled, and I killed it mid-run at 38 of 81 suites while it was working correctly; a monitoring probe that fails closed to a number indistinguishable from a real measurement is the same defect class this repo has been chasing, applied to a destructive decision instead of a green test","source":"reproduced 2026-08-20: find /tmp -name probe -newermt '-5 minutes' errors and yields 0 for a file created one second earlier; the killed agent's log full-copy-off.log had mtime 14:02Z while the check at 14:02Z reported no writes for 8 minutes"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-20T16:02:54Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-nested-isolation-per-suite-cost","ref_id":"suites-run-in-a-disposable-worktree","severity":"P3","finding":"suites that invoke a funnel themselves pay the disposable-checkout cost again, so 9 of 81 suites exceed the 2s-per-suite bound individually (worst +20s on aai-delta-stage3) while the mean is 1.07s","decision":"AC-006 is met on the mean and on the primitive, but the per-suite bound is read as a per-suite promise and nesting breaks it; a nested run could reuse the parent's checkout instead of making its own","source":"matched full-framework pair in scratchpad/aai-copy, run test-20260820-145025 (isolation OFF, 2382s) vs test-20260820-140915 (isolation ON, 2469s); one checkout cycle measured at 342ms mean over 20 iterations (scratchpad/iso-cost.tsv)"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-20T16:02:54Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-iso-kill-arms-double-covered","ref_id":"suites-run-in-a-disposable-worktree","severity":"P3","finding":"TEST-004's watchdog-kill and interrupt sub-arms cannot be falsified by any single-point mutation because cleanup on those paths is covered twice (the in-loop iso_destroy plus the EXIT/INT/TERM traps); only two-point mutations turn them red","decision":"a mutation-proof discipline that accepts double-covered assertions cannot tell a live guard from a dead one; either the redundancy is deliberate and recorded, or one cover should go","source":"bite proofs: MF6 (INT trap removed) and MF7 (TERM trap removed) both left the whole suite green; MFB (EXIT+TERM removed) reddened TEST-004(c); MFD (EXIT+INT traps plus the in-loop destroy removed) reddened TEST-004(d) — logs in scratchpad/bite/"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-20T17:38:32Z","actor":"remediation","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-iso-wroot-string-compare-dead","ref_id":"suites-run-in-a-disposable-worktree","severity":"P3","finding":"TEST-001 and TEST-005(a)/(c) compare the fixture root as a STRING, so on macOS those sub-assertions can never fire","decision":"new_fixture builds the path from $TMPDIR, which ends in a slash on macOS, so $d carries a // that the suite's own cd-normalised pwd has already collapsed; the != comparison is therefore always true and the 'ran IN the shipping repository' branch is unreachable. The arms still bite through their repo_state before/after pair, and on Linux (TMPDIR unset, /tmp) the comparison does work - so this is a macOS-only dead assertion, not a false green. TEST-005(e) added in remediation uses cd+pwd -P on both sides and is unaffected","source":"measured 2026-08-20 during the R-3 bite proof: with the suffix-glob mutation restored, TEST-005(e) reported MOVED but the string form of the same check stayed silent; wroot=/var/folders/.../T/manual-fix.jC9l5w vs d=/var/folders/.../T//manual-fix.jC9l5w"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-20T17:39:02Z","actor":"remediation","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-worktree-shares-git-admin-surface","ref_id":"suites-run-in-a-disposable-worktree","severity":"P2","finding":"a suite in a disposable worktree can still write the shipping repository's refs, .git/config and .git/hooks, because a worktree shares one .git","decision":"isolation protects the WORKING TREE only; neither git status --porcelain=v1 nor git rev-parse HEAD nor the tripwire looks at the administrative surface, so a tag, a branch, a config key and an executable post-checkout hook all land unobserved. The hook case is a persistent-code-execution shape. Closing it needs a separate object store and a scrubbed .git per suite","source":"validation round 1 finding F1, docs/ai/validation/validation-20260820T164823Z-suites-run-in-a-disposable-worktree-round1.md; the spec Summary and D7 were corrected in remediation to stop overclaiming, the behaviour was not changed"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-20T17:39:02Z","actor":"remediation","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-worktree-hook-disarms-later-suites","ref_id":"suites-run-in-a-disposable-worktree","severity":"P2","finding":"via the shared .git, one suite can arm a failing post-checkout hook that makes git worktree add fail, so every LATER suite in the run executes in the live tree","decision":"the second-order consequence of fu-worktree-shares-git-admin-surface: isolation silently stops arming mid-run. Mitigated twice at runtime (a per-suite log_warn naming the degrade, then the still-armed tripwire failing the run), which is why it is filed rather than blocking","source":"validation round 1 finding F2, docs/ai/validation/validation-20260820T164823Z-suites-run-in-a-disposable-worktree-round1.md"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-20T18:32:16Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-filed-list-trusted-again","ref_id":"suites-run-in-a-disposable-worktree","severity":"P2","finding":"the orchestrator again treated a role's 'filed:' list as proof a registry entry exists; fu-iso-suite-leaks-its-own-fixture-dirs was cited as filed in a dispatch and in a decision, and grep of decisions.jsonl returns zero","decision":"this is a recurrence of fu-suggested-ids-read-as-filed, opened one day earlier for the identical mistake, so the lesson did not survive a single day; a disposition of 'filed rather than fixed' that rests on a nonexistent filing is a silent drop dressed as a decision","source":"code review NB-6, suites-run-in-a-disposable-worktree, 2026-08-20"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-20T18:45:13Z","actor":"remediation","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-isolation-arm-failure-uncounted","ref_id":"suites-run-in-a-disposable-worktree","severity":"P1","finding":"nothing COUNTS a failure to arm isolation: no counter, no summary line, no run-ledger field — all three degrade paths are a single mid-scroll log_warn","decision":"HARD PRECONDITION for deleting the tripwire (D8). While the tripwire is armed, a run where isolation silently never armed still turns red on the first write; once it is deleted, that run stays GREEN and nothing in the output or the ledger says isolation was off. The three paths are test-framework.sh iso_probe (ISOLATION_WHY set, never surfaced as a count), the per-suite 'no disposable checkout for X' log_warn, and the 'X is not in the disposable checkout' log_warn; aai-run-tests.sh has the matching AAI-ISOLATION NOTE on stderr. Needs a per-run armed/degraded count, a summary line, and a run-ledger field, all three, before the tripwire goes.","source":"code review NB-2, suites-run-in-a-disposable-worktree, 2026-08-20; remediation pass 2 confirmed the three log_warn sites at tests/skills/test-framework.sh:282,515,521"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-20T18:45:29Z","actor":"remediation","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-iso-bases-reset-discards-entries","ref_id":"suites-run-in-a-disposable-worktree","severity":"P2","finding":"ISOLATION_BASES=() resets the array WHOLESALE at tests/skills/test-framework.sh (run_test, both the seeding-missed branch and the post-run destroy), discarding any entry it did not create","decision":"The array is what the EXIT/INT/TERM/HUP traps drain. A wholesale reset is only correct while exactly one checkout is ever live; the moment a second exists the reset drops a live registration+directory from the trap's view. The paired hole: iso_create appends to ISOLATION_BASES BEFORE seeding, so a set -e abort between the append and ISO_LAST_WT leaves the base registered but the caller sees failure and never destroys it — and the next wholesale reset then discards it, leaking both a directory and a git worktree registration past the EXIT trap. Fix is to remove the specific base rather than reset, and to make iso_create's failure path destroy what it registered.","source":"code review NB-3, suites-run-in-a-disposable-worktree, 2026-08-20; sites tests/skills/test-framework.sh:517,535 and iso_create's append at :278"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-20T18:45:29Z","actor":"remediation","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-iso-wrapper-traps-dont-reap-group","ref_id":"suites-run-in-a-disposable-worktree","severity":"P2","finding":"the wrapper's INT/TERM/HUP traps (.aai/scripts/aai-run-tests.sh) call aai_iso_cleanup and exit WITHOUT reaping the wrapped command's process group first","decision":"Ctrl-C therefore deletes the disposable checkout out from under a suite that is still running in it: the trap fires, worktree remove --force plus rm -rf take the tree away, the wrapper exits 130, and the orphaned suite keeps executing with its cwd unlinked — writing into a deleted directory, or worse resolving a relative path against whatever remains. The wrapper already knows how to reap a group (the setsid/pgid machinery below the traps); the traps just do not use it. Order must be kill the group, wait, then clean up.","source":"code review NB-4, suites-run-in-a-disposable-worktree, 2026-08-20; traps at .aai/scripts/aai-run-tests.sh:349-351"}
{"v": 1, "ts": "2026-08-20T18:45:47Z", "actor": "remediation", "type": "process_finding", "ref_id": "suites-run-in-a-disposable-worktree", "finding": "fu-iso-suite-leaks-its-own-fixture-dirs was cited as a filed follow-up in an orchestrator dispatch and in a recorded decision, and it was never filed: grep of docs/ai/decisions.jsonl returns the id only inside the text of fu-filed-list-trusted-again, never as an entry of its own. Recorded here so the citation is not left dangling: the id is NOT being opened as a follow-up, because the defect it named was FIXED in remediation pass 2 instead of tracked. The defect: tests/skills/test-aai-suite-isolation.sh new_fixture appended to a WORKDIRS array, but all fifteen call sites invoke it through a command substitution, so the append happened in a subshell and the EXIT trap drained an empty list. Measured on this machine before the fix: 17 fixture directories leaked per clean run and 374 had accumulated; after the fix, a clean run leaves a delta of 0. The fix replaces the array with an append to a registry FILE, which crosses the subshell boundary. This is the identical defect D4 already documents as measured-and-fixed inside iso_create, in the suite that exists to prove cleanup.", "decision": "Do not open fu-iso-suite-leaks-its-own-fixture-dirs. The defect is closed in code with a before/after directory count as evidence; the recurrence of citing an unfiled id is already tracked as fu-filed-list-trusted-again (P2, opened 2026-08-20).", "source": "code review NB-6 and NB-7, suites-run-in-a-disposable-worktree, 2026-08-20; remediation pass 2 evidence: pre-fix control run delta=17, post-fix run delta=0, both counted as find $TMPDIR -maxdepth 1 -name 'aai-isolation-*'"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-20T19:29:53Z","actor":"remediation","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-prune-repair-error-string-misquoted","ref_id":"spec-suites-run-in-a-disposable-worktree","severity":"P3","finding":"the iso_deregister comments in test-framework.sh and aai-run-tests.sh attribute 'fatal: not a git repository' to 'git worktree repair'; repair actually answers 'error: unable to locate repository', which is what the spec quotes","decision":"the comment is the only in-code record of why the repository-wide prune was rejected, and a reader who reproduces it gets a different string than the one quoted, which reads as the claim not reproducing","source":"measured 2026-08-20 on git 2.50.1 (Apple Git-155) in a throwaway repo: prune -v deregistered an unreachable-but-alive worktree; 'git worktree repair <path>' then printed 'error: unable to locate repository; .git file does not reference a repository: <path>' rc=1, while a git command run from INSIDE the restored directory printed 'fatal: not a git repository: <admin path>' rc=128 — two commands, two strings"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-20T19:43:46Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-ac-flip-must-precede-close","ref_id":"suites-run-in-a-disposable-worktree","severity":"P3","finding":"the AC Status rows must be flipped to a terminal state BEFORE close-work-item.mjs runs, not after; the orchestrator has been dispatching the opposite order","decision":"close-work-item's own post-close audit rejects a document going to status done while its AC rows are non-terminal (cls=drifted verdict=probable-false-done) and rolls the close back, so the documented order simply does not work and every ride rediscovers it","source":"PR ceremony for CHANGE-0152, 2026-08-20; first close attempt failed and rolled back cleanly, flip-then-close succeeded with docs-audit --gate CLEAN"}
